The [ United States federal government
](/wiki/United_States_federal_government "United States federal government")
has continuously had a fluctuating [ public debt ](/wiki/Government_debt
"Government debt") since its formation in 1789, except for about a year during
1835–1836, a period in which the nation, during the presidency of [ Andrew
Jackson ](/wiki/Andrew_Jackson "Andrew Jackson") , completely paid the
national debt. To allow comparisons over the years, public debt is often
expressed as a ratio to [ GDP ](/wiki/GDP "GDP") . The United States public
debt as a percentage of GDP reached its highest level during [ Harry Truman
](/wiki/Harry_S._Truman "Harry S. Truman") 's first presidential term, during
and after [ World War II ](/wiki/World_War_II "World War II") . Public debt as
a percentage of GDP fell rapidly in the [ post-World War II period
](/wiki/Post%E2%80%93World_War_II_economic_expansion "Post–World War II
economic expansion") and reached a low in 1974 under [ Richard Nixon
](/wiki/Richard_Nixon "Richard Nixon") . Debt as a share of GDP has
consistently increased since then, except during the presidencies of [ Jimmy
Carter ](/wiki/Jimmy_Carter "Jimmy Carter") and [ Bill Clinton
](/wiki/Bill_Clinton "Bill Clinton") .

Public debt rose sharply during the 1980s, as [ Ronald Reagan
](/wiki/Ronald_Reagan "Ronald Reagan") negotiated with [ Congress
](/wiki/United_States_Congress "United States Congress") to cut tax rates and
increase [ military spending ](/wiki/Military_budget_of_the_United_States
"Military budget of the United States") . It fell during the 1990s because of
decreased military spending, increased taxes and the [ 1990s boom
](/wiki/1990s_United_States_boom "1990s United States boom") . Public debt
rose sharply during [ George W. Bush's presidency
](/wiki/Presidency_of_George_W._Bush "Presidency of George W. Bush") and in
the wake of the [ 2007–2008 financial crisis
](/wiki/Financial_crisis_of_2007%E2%80%932008 "Financial crisis of 2007–2008")
, with resulting significant tax revenue declines and spending increases, such
as the [ Emergency Economic Stabilization Act of 2008

